  "text": "She lied to the king's men, hid two spies under flax on her roof, and negotiated her whole family out of a war. Joshua two. Two Israelite spies come into Jericho and end up at the house of a woman named Rahab. The text says exactly what she is. It does not soften it and neither will I. The king finds out and sends men to bring them out. And Rahab hides them. Then looks the king's messengers in the face and says, \"There came men unto me, but I wist not whence they were.\" Then sends them chasing in the wrong direction. She lied to a king. The Bible writes it down without comment. Then she goes up to the roof and tells the spies the actual reason. \"I know that the Lord hath given you the land.\" She had heard what happened at the Red Sea. She says, \"our hearts did melt.\" And then, \"The Lord your God, he is God in heaven above, and in earth beneath.\" That is a confession of faith from a Canaanite sex worker, and it is sharper theology than most of Israel manages that decade. Then she negotiates. And notice what she negotiates for. Not herself. \"Save alive my father, and my mother, and my brethren, and my sisters.\" Scarlet cord in the window. Her whole household lives. And Matthew puts her in the family line of Jesus.",
